m, a seat that has favored Republicans by about five points. First-term Republican Jeff Hurd seeks reelection against Democrat Dwayne Romero, an Aspen-area businessman, with a Libertarian and an independent also on the ballot. Hurd holds a commanding financial advantage, having raised $3.6 million to Romero's $717,000, and enters the fall with $1.8 million in the bank.\n\nRatings from nine national analysts split between Lean Republican and Likely Republican, and prediction markets give Romero roughly a 37 percent chance. Public polling is sparse; the only recent head-to-head, a July survey, showed Hurd ahead by a single point. A favorable national climate for Democrats and Hurd's emphasis on disaster relief and independence from his party keep the race competitive, but the district's lean and the money gap favor the incumbent.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.2665, "margin": -3.72}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2864, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.288, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.3508, "margin": -3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.2424, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.2636, "margin": -3.5}]}